# Who reviewed what

This section records who reviewed the paper, under whose instructions, and what each review caught and missed. It is written for a reader who wants to reproduce the record, so it keeps what is documented separate from what is not, and it does not upgrade any reviewer pass into something it was not. One caveat belongs up front: neither panel described here is peer review in the ordinary sense. The first panel was not independent of the institution that produced the paper, and its members were not blind to one another. The second panel was run by the author, on the author's own tooling, under the author's briefs. The only contributor in this record who stood outside both panels is an outside reader who reviewed one version, described below.

The first panel consisted of three LLM reviewer agents carrying methods, economics, and multi-agent-systems personas. The hub dispatched them, and the briefs they worked from were written by the hub. The panel ran for five rounds. From round 3 to round 5, its work ran through the instrument on the same free-lane worker that drafted most of the paper. That overlap between drafter and reviewer is one of the reasons the label peer review does not apply. All five rounds of transcripts are public in the companion repository under reviews/, which means a reader can read every exchange in full, including the exchanges where the panel failed. What the public record does not state, and what this section will not supply, is when the panel sat or which versions of the paper it reviewed: no dates and no version numbers for the first panel appear in the record behind this section.

The panel's catches were concrete. In round 1 it flagged a retracted statistic that was still live in the abstract. In round 3, reviewer A reported a turn count that had been written from memory, and the figure did not reproduce under the reviewer's own query. In round 4, reviewers A and B re-ran the published query at the hub's instruction and found an unverified query anchor. Two of the three catches share a mechanism: the number in the text had not been re-derived, and it failed as soon as a reviewer ran the query for itself.

The misses matter as much as the catches. A fabricated worked example passed six reviewer passes before a hub-side record check caught it. The record attributes those six passes to no specific rounds or panels; it states only that the example survived six passes in total. The lesson the record supports is narrow. The reviewer passes did not catch this fabrication, and the check that did was performed against records on the hub side, outside the panel process. A reader who treats reviewer coverage as a fabrication control is not supported by this history.

The second panel was a different construction. It comprised four reviewer personas and a chair drawn from a different model family. It was run by the author's own review tooling, and its briefs were written by the author. It also ran five rounds, on 13 and 14 October 2026, against versions v1.5, v1.6, v1.7.6, v1.7.7 and v1.8. Unlike the first panel, its transcripts are not in the companion repository, and the record behind this section does not say where they are held, so a reader cannot inspect these exchanges the way the first panel's can be inspected. The record also does not name the model families involved, describe the four personas' remits, or say which version each round examined.

What the second panel supplied, before anything else, was error. It produced a wrong Table 1 total, 860 against a correct 840, which the author transcribed into the paper; the same panel caught the mistake one round later. It also supplied four further sentences, and the same panel caught those two rounds later. In both cases the panel that supplied the defect was also the panel that caught it, and in the first case the author's transcription stood in between. A reader weighing this panel's contribution should weigh that loop, not only the corrections that came out of it.

Between the two panel histories sits one review with no panel behind it. An outside reader, not a member of either panel, reviewed v1.7.9 and sent five findings. Four were applied, and the result was v1.8. The fifth, a proposal to move reconstruction detail into a supplement, was deferred. The record does not name the reader, describe how the version reached them, or enumerate the four applied findings, and this section leaves those as gaps rather than filling them.

Across the whole history, the record supports a summary about checks rather than about reviewers. Two incidents were caught by the hub's provenance audit. Three hub-side number errors were caught by the reviewers, and the audit missed all three. The record does not say which two incidents the audit caught, and this section will not guess. Taken case by case: the retracted statistic, the memory-written turn count and the unverified query anchor were caught by the first panel in rounds 1, 3 and 4. The fabricated worked example slipped past six reviewer passes and fell to a hub-side record check. The wrong Table 1 total and the four further sentences came from the second panel and were caught by that same panel, one round later and two rounds later respectively. No single check was sufficient.

For reproduction, the entry points are uneven. The first panel can be audited directly: its five rounds of transcripts are public under reviews/, and the three catches named above should be findable in them at the rounds given. The second panel cannot be audited from the companion repository at all. The outside reader's review is recorded here only in summary: five findings sent, four applied as v1.8, one deferred. Everything else a reproducer would want is missing from the record behind this section: dates and versions for the first panel, model identities for both panels, the definition of the instrument, the location of the second panel's transcripts, the name of the outside reader, and the text of the four applied findings. Those gaps are stated here so the reader knows the boundary of this record, and so that nothing invisible gets filled in later.

